\subsection{Source-Reachable and Path-Dependent Lyapunov Refinement}
\label{sec:stability-widening-source-path}

The envelope theorem of Sec.~\ref{sec:stability-widening-phase-a} is deliberately
strong: it allows every predictable switching sequence in the compact family
$\Pi^{\rm env}$, even if no execution of the estimator can realize that
sequence.  This robustness statement is retained unchanged.  For quantitative
certification of the deployed source, however, it is unnecessarily conservative
to combine arbitrary parameter jumps, pseudo-update phases, covariance states,
magnetic states, and accepted-update patterns that are individually admissible
but mutually unreachable.  This subsection adds a second route that uses only
source-reachable words and permits transient Lyapunov growth inside a certified
word provided the complete word contracts.

The homogeneous held-bias and active-bias systems have different active
coordinates (18 and 21 states).  The constructions below are therefore applied
separately to each fixed-dimensional normal-Live mode
$m\in\{H,A\}$.  Bias release, hard magnetic re-gauging, and tilt re-lock are not
silently multiplied into a fixed-dimensional homogeneous word; their exact
source maps remain covered by the finite-capture and hybrid results of
Secs.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture} and \ref{sec:hybrid-live-stability}.

\subsubsection{Source-reachable finite-window contraction}

For a starting sample $k$, integer $N\ge1$, and fixed normal-Live mode $m$, let
$\Pi^{{\rm src},m}_{k,N}$ denote the compact family of length-$N$ proof
sequences that can be generated by the implemented estimator from the declared
reachable set without leaving mode $m$.  Each sequence contains the same
objects used elsewhere in the proof: pseudo-measurement phase, magnetic gauge
state when it is not undergoing a hard rewrite, accepted-update pattern,
covariance recursion, staged or Live OU/$R_S$ parameters, and all source guards
that determine the homogeneous matrix.  By construction,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\Pi^{{\rm src},m}_{k,N}\subseteq\Pi^{{\rm env},m}_{k,N}.}
 \label{eq:widen-src-subset}
\end{equation}
Let $\widetilde\Psi_m(k+N,k;\pi)$ be the corresponding homogeneous
dimensionless transition and define
\begin{equation}
 \kappa_{N,m}^{\rm src}:=
 \sup_k\sup_{\pi\in\Pi^{{\rm src},m}_{k,N}}
 \|\widetilde\Psi_m(k+N,k;\pi)\|,
 \qquad
 \kappa_{N,m}^{\rm env}:=
 \sup_k\sup_{\pi\in\Pi^{{\rm env},m}_{k,N}}
 \|\widetilde\Psi_m(k+N,k;\pi)\|.
 \label{eq:widen-src-kappa}
\end{equation}
Then Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-subset} gives immediately
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\kappa_{N,m}^{\rm src}\le\kappa_{N,m}^{\rm env}.}
 \label{eq:widen-src-monotone}
\end{equation}
Thus the source-specific contraction test can pass even when a rigorous
arbitrary-envelope bound is inconclusive.

\begin{theorem}[Source-reachable finite-window UES]
\label{thm:widen-src-kappa-ues}
Assume the physical and covariance compactness hypotheses of
Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-envelope-ues} for a fixed mode $m$.  If, for some finite
$N$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\kappa_{N,m}^{\rm src}<1,}
 \label{eq:widen-src-kappa-condition}
\end{equation}
then the homogeneous recursion in mode $m$ is uniformly exponentially stable
over its source-reachable family.  Individual samples inside the $N$-step
window may have norm gain greater than one.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At lifted indices $k_j=k_0+jN$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-kappa-condition}
gives
$\|z_{k_{j+1}}\|\le\kappa_{N,m}^{\rm src}\|z_{k_j}\|$.  Iteration yields
geometric contraction at the lifted indices.  On the compact source family,
$\bar A_{{\rm src},m}:=\sup\|\widetilde A_{k,m}^{\rm cl}\|<\infty$, so the at
most $N-1$ intermediate samples are bounded by
$\bar A_{{\rm src},m}^{N-1}$.  Combining the lifted contraction and finite
prefix bound gives UES at every sample of that normal-Live segment.
\end{proof}

The theorem is an additive refinement, not a replacement for
Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-envelope-ues}.  Whenever the arbitrary-envelope
certificate verifies $\kappa_{N,m}^{\rm env}<1$, the source certificate passes
by Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-monotone}.  The converse need not hold because the
envelope permits words that the source never generates.

\subsubsection{Finite source graph and path-dependent metrics}

A single Euclidean norm or a single common quadratic metric can still be
conservative because each fixed-dimensional source mode has distinct proof
phases.  For mode $m$, introduce a finite proof graph
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr G_{{\rm src},m}=(\mathscr M_m,\mathscr E_m)
 \label{eq:widen-src-graph}
\end{equation}
whose nodes cover the compact source-reachable proof state within that mode.  A
node may encode, for example, pseudo-update phase, magnetic state, and a
declared compact cell of covariance and scheduled parameters.  Each edge
$e=(i,j)\in\mathscr E_m$ carries the compact square-matrix family
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr A_e:=\left\{\widetilde A_{k,m}^{\rm cl}:
 \text{the source can move from node $i$ to node $j$ in one sample}\right\}.
 \label{eq:widen-src-edge-family}
\end{equation}
The graph is an outer cover of the source in mode $m$, not of the full
$\Pi^{\rm env}$: every actual same-mode source transition must lie on an edge,
while edges known to be unreachable are excluded.  Continuous covariance and
tuning values remain inside the compact edge families and are not discretized
as point samples.  A transition that changes the active state dimension is a
boundary between such graphs and is handled by the already-declared exact
source transition map, not by Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-edge-family}.

For a graph word
$w=(i_0,e_0,i_1,\ldots,e_{r-1},i_r)$, let
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr P_w:=\left\{
 \mat\Phi_w=\mat A_{r-1}\cdots\mat A_0:
 \mat A_j\in\mathscr A_{e_j}
 \text{ and the complete tuple is source-reachable}\right\}.
 \label{eq:widen-src-word-family}
\end{equation}
Retaining complete source reachability in $\mathscr P_w$ prevents another
independent-supremum relaxation between successive edges.

Let $\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}$ be a finite collection of such words with lengths
$\ell(w)$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 1\le \ell_-\le\ell(w)\le\ell_+<\infty.
 \label{eq:widen-src-word-length}
\end{equation}
Call $\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}$ \emph{source complete} if every infinite
admissible source path that remains in mode $m$ can be tiled successively by
words from $\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}$.  The tiling need not be periodic and
different words may have different lengths.

Assign a symmetric matrix $\mat P_i\succ0$ to each graph node.  Define uniform
metric bounds
\begin{equation}
 p_-\mat I\preceq\mat P_i\preceq p_+\mat I,
 \qquad i\in\mathscr M_m.
 \label{eq:widen-path-P-bounds}
\end{equation}
For every certified word require
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mat\Phi_w^\top\mat P_{i_r}\mat\Phi_w-\mat P_{i_0}
 \preceq-\mat Q_w,
 \qquad
 \mat Q_w\succeq q_-\mat I,
 \quad q_->0,}
 \label{eq:widen-path-lmi}
\end{equation}
for every $\mat\Phi_w\in\mathscr P_w$.  This is a robust finite-word matrix
inequality.  A computer-assisted verifier must enclose the complete compact
matrix family; checking finitely many nominal trajectories alone is not a proof.

\begin{theorem}[Source-complete path Lyapunov UES]
\label{thm:widen-path-ues}
For a fixed mode $m$, suppose $\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}$ is source complete,
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-P-bounds}--\eqref{eq:widen-path-lmi} hold, and the
one-step source family is bounded.  Then the homogeneous recursion is UES on
every source-reachable execution that remains in that mode.  At certified word
boundaries,
\begin{equation}
 V^+\le\lambda_{\mathscr W}V^-,
 \qquad
 \lambda_{\mathscr W}:=1-\frac{q_-}{p_+}<1,
 \label{eq:widen-path-contraction}
\end{equation}
with $V=z^\top P_i z$.  No one-step decrease is required inside a word.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For a word beginning in node $i_0$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-lmi} gives
\begin{align}
 V^+-V^-
 &\le-q_-\|z^-\|^2
 \le-\frac{q_-}{p_+}V^- ,
\end{align}
which proves Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-contraction}.  Source completeness tiles
every infinite same-mode source execution by contracting words of uniformly
bounded length.  The compact one-step matrix family gives a finite amplification
bound for the at most $\ell_+-1$ samples between word boundaries.  Uniform
metric bounds then convert the word-boundary estimate to a sample-by-sample UES
estimate.
\end{proof}

If desired, replace $1-q_-/p_+$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-contraction} by any verified nonnegative upper bound on
the generalized word gain
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_w=
 \sup_{\Phi_w\in\mathscr P_w}
 \lambda_{\max}\!\left(
 P_{i_0}^{-1/2}\Phi_w^\top P_{i_r}\Phi_wP_{i_0}^{-1/2}
 \right)<1.
 \label{eq:widen-path-generalized-gain}
\end{equation}
This avoids converting every path to a common $q_-$ when separate path margins
are available.

\begin{remark}[Why the path metric can be strictly less conservative]
\label{rem:widen-path-dominance}
The Phase-B Euclidean $N$-step certificate is contained as the special choice
$P_i=I$ with fixed word length $N$ after restricting its word family to the
same mode.  A common quadratic metric is contained as the special choice
$P_i=P$ for every node.  The path certificate admits both $P_i\ne P_j$ and
multi-sample words, so a prediction or weak-update sample may increase one
metric while the complete source word decreases the appropriate endpoint
metric.  Therefore failure of a common one-step or arbitrary-envelope
certificate does not imply failure of Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-path-ues}.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{Constructive constants without the $\kappa_N\!\to M$ relaxation}

Let
\begin{equation}
 B_{\rm pre}:=
 \sup_{w\in\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}}
 \sup_{\substack{0\le s<\ell(w)\\
                  \text{source-reachable prefixes}}}
 \left\|
 P_{i_s}^{1/2}\Phi_{w,s}P_{i_0}^{-1/2}
 \right\|,
 \label{eq:widen-path-prefix}
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_{w,s}$ is the length-$s$ homogeneous prefix and the node index at
that prefix is $i_s$.  Compactness and the finite maximum word length imply
$B_{\rm pre}<\infty$.  Combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-contraction} and
\eqref{eq:widen-path-prefix} gives directly
\begin{equation}
 \|z_k\|
 \le
 \sqrt{\frac{p_+}{p_-}}\,B_{\rm pre}
 \lambda_{\mathscr W}^{\,n(k)/2}\|z_{k_0}\|,
 \label{eq:widen-path-ues-bound}
\end{equation}
where $n(k)$ is the number of completed certified words.  Since every word has
length at most $\ell_+$,
$n(k)\ge\lfloor(k-k_0)/\ell_+\rfloor-1$.  Hence
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-ues-bound} supplies explicit UES constants directly
from $(P_i,\lambda_w,B_{\rm pre},\ell_+)$ and does not require the conservative
intermediate chain
$\kappa_N\rightarrow\rho\rightarrow M\rightarrow p_+$ of the broad-envelope
construction.

\subsubsection{Lifted nonlinear direct-decrease certificate}

The same fixed-mode source words can be used in the nonlinear theorem.  For a
word $w\in\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}$ let
\begin{equation}
 z^+=\mathcal F_w(z,0)=\Phi_w z+\eta_w(z)
 \label{eq:widen-path-nonlinear-lift}
\end{equation}
be the exact zero-input lifted error map obtained by composing the implemented
prediction, all accepted measurement corrections, full $S=0$ cross-gain,
quaternion injection/reset, covariance recursion, and same-mode source
transitions along that word.  The accumulated nonlinear term $\eta_w$ is
defined by the identity in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-nonlinear-lift}; it is not
formed by independently maximizing one-step remainder norms.

For compact source-node tubes $\mathcal T_i$ whose complete sample prefixes are
safe, define
\begin{equation}
 \mu_{\Delta V}^{\rm path}:=
 \inf_{w\in\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}}
 \inf_{\substack{z\in\mathcal T_{i_0}\\z\ne0\\
                  \text{all prefixes safe}}}
 \frac{
 z^\top P_{i_0}z-
 \mathcal F_w(z,0)^\top P_{i_r}\mathcal F_w(z,0)}
 {\|z\|^2}.
 \label{eq:widen-path-direct-margin}
\end{equation}
All continuous covariance, geometry, gain, and parameter values inside the word
are restricted to the same source-reachable compact family used in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-word-family}.

\begin{theorem}[Source-word direct-decrease regional practical ISS]
\label{thm:widen-path-direct-iss}
For a fixed normal-Live mode $m$, assume a source-complete word family with safe
prefixes and
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\mu_{\Delta V}^{\rm path}>0.}
 \label{eq:widen-path-direct-condition}
\end{equation}
Then sufficiently small endpoint Lyapunov sublevels contained in the node tubes
are robustly invariant at certified word boundaries for sufficiently small
bounded deterministic input.  All intermediate samples remain in the declared
safe domain, and the resulting same-mode source execution satisfies a
practical-ISS estimate.  Positive one-step Lyapunov increments are permitted
inside a word.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-path-direct-condition} gives a strict exact zero-input
decrease over every admissible source word.  On the compact prefix-safe lifted
family, the disturbance-to-endpoint map is uniformly bounded and locally
Lipschitz in the disturbance.  Young inequalities therefore preserve a strict
word-level inward margin for a sufficiently small disturbance budget.  Iteration
over the source-complete tiling gives practical ISS at word boundaries, while
the finite prefix bound gives the sample-by-sample estimate and safety.
\end{proof}

The one-step direct theorem of
Sec.~\ref{sec:stability-widening-structured} is recovered in either fixed mode
when every certified word has length one and the node metric equals the
corresponding original $P_k$.  Therefore the source-word condition is an
additive widening route: a source tube that passes the one-step direct test is
also covered by the length-one specialization, while a longer source word may
still pass when some individual samples have positive $\Delta V$.

Mode changes are composed rather than hidden in this theorem.  In particular,
the held-to-active bias-release transition must place the post-transition error
inside a certified active-mode node tube (or the finite-capture inner set), and
subsequent active words then use the 21-state metric.  The same principle
applies to hard magnetic or tilt jumps through the existing hybrid recovery
conditions.

\subsubsection{Verification contract and scope}

A rigorous source-path certificate reports, for each fixed-dimensional mode,
at least
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \{\mathscr G_{{\rm src},m},\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m},
 P_i,p_-,p_+,\lambda_w,B_{\rm pre},\ell_+,
 \mu_{\Delta V}^{\rm path}\}.}
 \label{eq:widen-path-contract}
\end{equation}
Its deterministic pass conditions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.7em]
 \item every reachable same-mode source transition is covered by
       $\mathscr G_{{\rm src},m}$;
 \item $\mathscr W_{{\rm src},m}$ is source complete for executions that remain
       in that mode;
 \item every robust word matrix inequality or equivalent generalized-gain bound
       is verified over the complete continuous source-reachable family;
 \item all nonlinear word prefixes remain in their declared safe tubes; and
 \item the selected linear or nonlinear word margin is strict.
\end{enumerate}
Interval arithmetic, affine arithmetic, Taylor models, branch-and-bound, robust
LMIs over certified matrix enclosures, or another validated enclosure method may
be used.  Monte Carlo trajectories and dense sampling are diagnostics only and
cannot establish the suprema or infima above.

This refinement changes neither the estimator nor the adaptation law.  It does
not weaken the arbitrary-envelope theorem and it does not assume that the tuner
is stability critical.  Rather, it uses source reachability only to obtain a
less conservative quantitative certificate for each fixed-dimensional deployed
Live mode, with existing source transition theorems composing those modes.
